## Claim
Text: "Previous flu vaccines may have made President Donald Trump susceptible to COVID-19"
Claim author: Miriam Fauzia
Claim date: October 21, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Is there any peer-reviewed scientific evidence demonstrating that receiving an influenza vaccine increases a person's susceptibility to contracting SARS-CoV-2?
Answer: No, there is no peer-reviewed scientific evidence demonstrating that receiving an influenza vaccine increases a person's susceptibility to contracting SARS-CoV-2. According to the CDC, "the preponderance of evidence suggests... that influenza vaccination does not, in fact, make people more susceptible to other respiratory infections." While some anti-vaccine claims have pointed to a Department of Defense study to suggest a link, that research was conducted during the 2017-2018 flu season before SARS-CoV-2 was even discovered, meaning "no one in the experiment was confirmed to have the virus that causes COVID-19."

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/flu-vaccine-covid-buttar/

### What does the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) state regarding the relationship between receiving a flu vaccine and the risk of contracting COVID-19?
Answer: According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), there is no evidence that receiving a flu vaccine increases a person's risk of contracting other respiratory infections, including COVID-19. The CDC states that "the preponderance of evidence suggests that... influenza vaccination does not, in fact, make people more susceptible to other respiratory infections."

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/flu-vaccine-covid-buttar/

### Are there peer-reviewed scientific studies that suggest receiving an influenza vaccine might actually reduce the severity or risk of COVID-19 infection?
Answer: Yes, peer-reviewed scientific research suggests that receiving an influenza vaccine may actually reduce the risk and severity of COVID-19. A 2022 systematic review and meta-analysis of 23 published articles, published in the *American Journal of Preventive Medicine*, found that "influenza vaccination was associated with reduced risk of COVID-19 infection... and hospitalization." The study concluded that "influenza vaccination is associated with reduced susceptibility to or disease severity of COVID-19 and that influenza vaccination may reduce the risk of COVID-19 and improve clinical outcomes."

Source URL: https://pesquisa.bvsalud.org/global-literature-on-novel-coronavirus-2019-ncov/resource/pt/covidwho-1889175

### What is the scientific consensus on the phenomenon of "viral interference" concerning the influenza vaccine and coronaviruses?
Answer: The scientific consensus is that there is no strong scientific or medical evidence showing that the influenza vaccine makes people more susceptible to other respiratory viruses, including coronaviruses, through viral interference. According to the CDC, the "preponderance of evidence suggests that this is not a common or regular occurrence and that influenza vaccination does not, in fact, make people more susceptible to other respiratory infections." Furthermore, a U.S. Department of Defense study examining this phenomenon "showed little to no evidence supporting the association of virus interference and influenza vaccination."

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/flu-vaccine-covid-buttar/

### Did a specific scientific study, such as the January 2020 study by Greg G. Wolff on influenza vaccination and respiratory virus interference, conclude that flu vaccines increase the risk of contracting COVID-19?
Answer: No, the study by Greg G. Wolff did not conclude that flu vaccines increase the risk of contracting COVID-19. The study used data collected two years before the pandemic and examined seasonal coronaviruses that cause common colds, not SARS-CoV-2. According to the Military Health System, the study "does not show or suggest that influenza vaccination predisposes in any way, the potential for infection with the more severe forms of coronavirus, such as COVID-19."

Source URL: https://www.factcheck.org/2020/04/no-evidence-that-flu-shot-increases-risk-of-covid-19/

### How did public health experts and researchers respond to claims that the influenza vaccine makes individuals more vulnerable to COVID-19?
Answer: Public health experts and researchers rejected these claims, stating there is no scientific evidence connecting the flu shot to an increased risk of COVID-19. They pointed out that the military study used to back the claim actually looked at seasonal coronaviruses that cause common colds, not SARS-CoV-2, and was conducted before the COVID-19 pandemic. Infectious disease epidemiologist Edward Belongia stated, "Overall, we do not see evidence of virus interference that is sufficient to raise concerns about flu vaccination and COVID-19 risk," while the Military Health System confirmed that the study "does not show or suggest that influenza vaccination predisposes in any way, the potential for infection with the more severe forms of coronavirus, such as COVID-19." Experts continue to emphasize the importance of getting the flu vaccine to prevent co-infections and reduce strain on the healthcare system.

Source URL: https://www.factcheck.org/2020/04/no-evidence-that-flu-shot-increases-risk-of-covid-19/

### What was the medical consensus in October 2020 regarding the safety and necessity of getting a flu shot during the COVID-19 pandemic?
Answer: In October 2020, the medical consensus was that getting a flu shot was safe, necessary, and "more important this year than ever" due to the concurrent circulation of SARS-CoV-2. Experts and the CDC strongly recommended getting vaccinated by the end of October, clarifying that "there’s no evidence that getting a flu shot will increase your susceptibility to COVID-19." Additionally, medical professionals emphasized that healthcare facilities were "implementing a tremendous amount of protective measures"—such as masking, screening, and social distancing—to ensure people could get vaccinated without risking exposure to COVID-19.

Source URL: https://www.consumerreports.org/health/misconceptions-about-flu-shot-and-covid-19-a7059031820/

### Are there any documented clinical cases where a patient's COVID-19 infection was medically proven to be caused or exacerbated by a prior influenza vaccination?
Answer: Based on the provided study, there are no documented clinical cases where a patient's COVID-19 infection was medically proven to be caused or exacerbated by a prior influenza vaccination. Instead, the research analyzed clinical data and concluded that "influenza vaccination does not increase the incidence of COVID-19 or worsen the related morbidity or mortality," finding no association between receiving a flu vaccine and an increased risk of contracting SARS-CoV-2 or experiencing severe COVID-19 outcomes.

