### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence demonstrates that there is no scientific or medical basis for the claim that influenza vaccines increase a person's susceptibility to COVID-19. Public health authorities, including the CDC, and medical experts have clarified that flu vaccines do not make individuals more vulnerable to other respiratory infections, including SARS-CoV-2. In fact, peer-reviewed research, including a 2022 systematic review, suggests that influenza vaccination may actually be associated with a reduced risk and severity of COVID-19. Claims to the contrary often rely on a misinterpreted 2017-2018 Department of Defense study that examined seasonal common cold coronaviruses before SARS-CoV-2 even existed. Because the scientific consensus and clinical data clearly show no link between flu vaccination and increased COVID-19 susceptibility, the evidence is highly clear and no further investigation is required.

### Conclusion
Based on the recorded evidence, the claim that previous flu vaccines may have made Donald Trump more susceptible to COVID-19 is completely contradicted by established scientific research and expert consensus. Multiple studies and public health organizations have confirmed that influenza vaccination does not increase the risk of contracting SARS-CoV-2, and may instead offer protective benefits against severe COVID-19 outcomes. Therefore, the decision is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
There is no scientific or medical evidence supporting the assertion that receiving a flu vaccine increases susceptibility to COVID-19. According to the [CDC](https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/flu-vaccine-covid-buttar/), influenza vaccination does not make individuals more vulnerable to other respiratory infections. In fact, a 2022 systematic review published in the [*American Journal of Preventive Medicine*](https://pesquisa.bvsalud.org/global-literature-on-novel-coronavirus-2019-ncov/resource/pt/covidwho-1889175) suggests that flu vaccination is associated with a reduced risk and severity of COVID-19 infection. Misleading claims to the contrary often misinterpret a pre-pandemic Department of Defense study on seasonal coronaviruses, which [public health experts](https://www.factcheck.org/2020/04/no-evidence-that-flu-shot-increases-risk-of-covid-19/) and the Military Health System confirmed does not show any link to increased COVID-19 risk. Furthermore, clinical data published in the [*Journal of Clinical and Translational Science*](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-clinical-and-translational-science/article/safety-of-influenza-vaccine-during-covid19/C26A417046677E65D421AD33B3E6281F) confirms that prior influenza vaccination does not increase COVID-19 incidence or worsen its clinical outcomes.